OpenXR Simulator @PROJECT_VERSION@

Launch and inspect OpenXR applications without a headset. The desktop launcher,
native MCP connector and bundled Luau automation need neither Python nor Node.
This is a preview package; see share/doc/simxr/RELEASE_QUALIFICATION.md for
tested platforms and limitations, and BUILDING.md in that directory for details.

GET STARTED

Windows: use the setup program for a per-user install and Start menu shortcut,
or extract the portable ZIP and open bin/simxr_companion.exe.
The Windows package targets native x64 Windows; ARM64 emulation is not supported.
Preview packages are unsigned.
Linux: extract the archive and run ./install.sh for application-menu integration,
or open ./bin/simxr_companion directly. Both platforms need a compatible Vulkan
driver and the system Vulkan loader; graphics drivers are not bundled.

Releases: https://github.com/Crementif/OpenXR-Simulator/releases
Support:  https://github.com/Crementif/OpenXR-Simulator

Open OpenXR Simulator, choose Add application, select an executable and Launch.
New profiles select the included runtime automatically. For AI/automation setup,
use Preferences > Copy MCP configuration, or run the native connector below:

  Windows PowerShell: .\bin\simxr_mcp_connector.exe --print-config --windowless
  Linux shell:        ./bin/simxr_mcp_connector --print-config --windowless

Omit --windowless to have the connector open the simulator window. Neither the
simulator nor the target executable needs to be running before the client starts.

INSTALLATION AND UNATTENDED SETUP

Extract the entire archive into a new directory. Keep bin/openxr_simulator.json
beside its runtime library; its relative path allows moving the package.

On Linux, run ./install.sh to copy the package to the current user's data
directory and add an application-menu entry. The package remains usable without
installation. Use ./install.sh --prefix /absolute/path for a custom location.
To remove an installation, run its install.sh --uninstall, adding the same
--prefix when one was specified. The script returns 0 on success, 2 when files
are in use or installation is locked, and 1 for other errors. Close both the
simulator and applications using its runtime before upgrading or uninstalling.
The Linux installer requires /proc, flock and standard GNU command-line
utilities. Current installations use a shared lock held by the app and loaded
runtime. Earlier experimental v1 installations require process inspection
before upgrade or uninstall; Linux may deny that check for unrelated processes.

The Windows setup installs per-user without administrator rights and creates a
Start menu shortcut. A desktop shortcut is optional. The final setup page offers
Launch OpenXR Simulator; unattended installation never launches it. For unattended setup, run:

  openxr-simulator-@PROJECT_VERSION@-windows-x64-setup.exe /VERYSILENT /SUPPRESSMSGBOXES /NORESTART /SP- /LOG="setup.log"

To uninstall silently, run unins000.exe from the installed directory:

  unins000.exe /VERYSILENT /SUPPRESSMSGBOXES /NORESTART /LOG="uninstall.log"

Setup returns 0 on success and 7 if preparation rejects the install, including
when files are in use. Uninstall returns 0 on success and a nonzero code on
failure; a running simulator or application using its runtime returns 1. The
log explains a refusal. The uninstaller can return before its second process
finishes; wait for its uninstall registration and .simxr-install marker to
disappear before treating removal as complete. Close those programs and retry;
setup does not close them or schedule binary replacements on reboot. User data
is preserved.

LAUNCH PROFILES AND RUNTIME SELECTION

On Windows, launch bin/simxr_companion.exe. On Linux, launch bin/simxr_companion.
Choose Add application and select the application's executable. New profiles use
this package's runtime automatically; the profile editor also supports an
explicit runtime manifest. Runtime selection affects that child process only
when the OpenXR loader permits environment overrides.
Run Windows applications without elevation: elevated loaders ignore XR_RUNTIME_JSON.
For absolute Windows manifest paths containing non-ASCII characters, the
launcher uses an ASCII short path when available. Otherwise it can create a
private compatibility manifest in the user's temporary directory, pointing to
the original runtime. Some loaders still require an ASCII package path if the
temporary directory is not ASCII-addressable or the bounded cache is full.
See share/doc/simxr/BUILDING.md for the cache limits and safe cleanup.
For command-line launch, set XR_RUNTIME_JSON to the absolute manifest path in the
application's environment. This avoids system registration when the loader
permits environment overrides. On Linux, bin/launch-linux.py also launches and
attaches the companion.

MCP AND AUTOMATION

For an MCP client that supports a stdio server, open Preferences in the
app, select MCP > Client (Generic JSON or Codex), and use Copy MCP
configuration. Merge the generated entry into the client's MCP
configuration. Alternatively, set the server's command to the absolute path of
bin/simxr_mcp_connector (bin/simxr_mcp_connector.exe on Windows). The connector
starts the companion beside it when needed; neither Python nor Node is required.
For unattended configuration, run bin/simxr_mcp_connector --print-config
(or bin/simxr_mcp_connector.exe --print-config on Windows). It prints a JSON
client configuration without starting the simulator or changing settings. Add
--client codex for a TOML entry to merge into Codex config.toml. Add
--windowless to include that option, and --app ABSOLUTE_PATH to target a
development executable. Regenerate the configuration after moving the package.
Add --windowless as a connector argument to request a host without a visible
window. An already-running matching companion is reused, including a visible
one. The connector can launch an application through simulator_launch_application;
the application does not need to be running when the client connects.

An already-open companion must have MCP enabled in Preferences, or be started
with --mcp, to be discovered. The connector stays running and reconnects after a
companion restart, but does not repeatedly start the app after it stops. After
a rebuild, start the selected app with --mcp or --windowless. Tool-list refresh
depends on the client; the verified Codex workflow needs its experimental
mcp_tool_list_changed setting. See the automation guide for setup. Restart the MCP
connection to update the connector itself. Use simxr_mcp_connector --version
(.exe on Windows) to identify its build and connector compatibility protocol.
Tool additions do not require a new connector protocol. An incompatible app
reports that a compatible connector is needed. Closing the connector leaves the
companion and launched applications running. After moving a portable extraction,
update the connector path in the client's configuration. See
share/doc/simxr/AUTOMATION.md for examples, image access and current limitations.

PROTON AND PLATFORM REQUIREMENTS

For Proton, use bin/prepare-proton-prefix.py to create a private simulator prefix.
In the launch profile, select that prepared prefix as Private Proton prefix.
Launching registers the selected simulator runtime only
inside that prefix. This registration persists until you remove the private
prefix. Shared and default prefixes are refused. See BUILDING.md for commands.

The Linux x64 package targets updated Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, with its C/C++ runtime,
X11 desktop libraries, the system Vulkan loader (libvulkan1), and a compatible
GPU driver. Other distributions may work but are not qualified. Wayland and
hardware compatibility remain separate checks. The optional headset host and
OpenXR probes need the system OpenXR loader (libopenxr-loader1); update checks
also need a compatible system libcurl.so.4. This archive does not install or
replace drivers. Windows requires a Vulkan-capable driver even when the
application uses Direct3D or OpenGL.

bin/simxr_companion --version
bin/simxr_runner --version

OPTIONAL SDK AND EXAMPLES

Python 3.10+ SDK: install share/simxr/sdk/python into a virtual environment with
pip, or put that directory on PYTHONPATH. Examples are in share/simxr/examples;
Luau rigs are also in share/simxr/rigs. The C game bridge header is in include.
See share/doc/simxr for runner and control protocol instructions. Optional loader
probes are in bin when built with SIMXR_BUILD_PROBES.

UPGRADES AND REMOVAL

Close the app, MCP connector and all applications using this package's runtime
before upgrading. Windows users can run the newer setup against the same install
location. Linux users can run install.sh from the new extracted package, passing
the existing --prefix if customized. For a portable upgrade, extract into a new
directory and regenerate the MCP configuration for its connector path.

To remove a portable extraction, close its processes and delete that directory.
Remove any XR_RUNTIME_JSON setting you added to your own launch scripts. User
settings and recordings are outside the package and are preserved. Extraction
and normal Windows/Linux launch do not change the machine's default OpenXR
runtime. To remove the Proton registration, close the prefix's applications and
delete only the private compatibility-data directory you prepared.
